      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Assess the effect of aspiration thrombectomy on diagnosis and management of embolic acute myocardial infarction."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Discrimination of embolic acute myocardial infarction from atherosclerotic plaque rupture/erosion prompts oral anticoagulation treatment of source of embolus, as well as avoiding unnecessary stenting and dual antiplatelet therapy. However, detection is difficult without aspiration."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We compared rates of diagnosis of embolic infarction for 2.5 years prior to (pre-RAT) and 2.5 years post routine aspiration thrombectomy (post-RAT). Baseline demographics, outcomes, and treatment strategies were also compared between the embolic infarction and atherosclerotic infarction."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Diagnosed embolic infarction rose from 1.2% in the pre-RAT era to 2.8% in the post-RAT period (P\u2009<\u20090.05). In addition, more successful removal of thrombus by aspiration led to less stenting (20% vs. 55% P\u2009<\u20090.05) in the post-RAT period thus avoiding the hazards of \"triple therapy.\" Embolic infarction was more frequently associated with atrial fibrillation (55% vs. 8%), had higher mortality (17% vs. 4%), and had higher rates of embolic stroke (13% vs. 0.3%) when compared with atherosclerotic MI (all P\u2009<\u20090.05)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Routine aspiration thrombectomy more readily identifies embolic infarction allowing more specific therapy and avoidance of stenting and triple anticoagulant therapy."
          }
        ]
      },
